#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devbox-offbox-watch: watch sami-devbox from off it, and restart what the registry asks.

    devbox-offbox-watch [--host HOST] [--registry DIR] [--train DIR] [--remote-proc DIR]
                        [--sre TOPIC|ID] [--chief TOPIC|ID] [--verified-linux-lead TOPIC|ID]
                        [--ssh-timeout S] [--state FILE] [--dry-run] [--report-only] [--once]
                        [--forced-command] [--ssh-key PATH] [--shadow-log PATH]

Every 5 minutes: the `devbox-offbox-watch` user timer on sami-rocqperf, and the CronJob
`devbox-offbox-watch` in the dispatch cluster that replaces it as that machine is shut down
(agent-infra `monitoring/offboxwatch.go`, dispatch://INFRA-993). It runs OFF the machine it
watches, which is the whole point: on 2026-11-04 sami-devbox stalled from about 16:40Z until a
cloud reset at 18:05:53Z, and every session, every in-session loop and every watcher ON it died
together while nothing off it noticed (dispatch://INFRA-993/ask/d13b86a5).

Two transports to the watched box. From sami-rocqperf the two remote halves travel over ssh
stdin, so they can never be a publish behind this file. From the cluster they cannot: the pod's
key carries `restrict,command="<pinned path>/devbox-offbox-act --forced"`, and a forced command
gets no stdin, so `--forced-command` sends a verb instead -- `probe`, `live <uuid>…`,
`restart <uuid>`, `await <uuid>` -- and the pinned acting half on the host reads a restart's
resume line from that session's registry entry rather than from here.

Three duties.

1. Session liveness, with restart. `<registry>/<session>.entry` holds, as plain key=value lines,
   how to bring one session back and what its absence costs; its owner writes and updates it
   (`/mnt/durable/sami/restart-registry/README.md`). For each entry with `auto_restart=yes` the
   watch asks `omp-session-live <id>` ON sami-devbox -- the only liveness test there is here,
   never an lsof on a transcript path, which on 11-03/04 reported a working lead dead and
   "restored" it four times. A dead one is resumed in a detached pane of the existing window
   named by `tmux=agents:<stream>`, from its `cwd`, then the window is tiled. Unplaced entries,
   legacy `dev` entries and missing stream windows use `agents:chief`; no session or window is
   created. When omp-session-live says it is live, its `after` message is sent.

2. The box itself. No ssh answer within --ssh-timeout on two runs in a row, or a writeback stall
   that holds for 10 minutes, alerts the SRE and the chief of staff.

3. The merge train (`--train`). Its operator, named in `runner.lock`, and the Verified Linux lead
   hear when the queue holds work and `events.tsv` has not moved for 30 minutes, when
   `runner.lock` names a pid that is not running, when `gate.lock` does, and when any of the
   three standing train processes (`operator/revert_watch.py`,
   `operator/nightly_accept_load.py`, `operator/snapshot.sh --loop`) is not running -- one alert
   per episode naming the whole missing set, and one line when they are all back. Each of those
   facts has three states, not two: running, gone, and unreadable. An unreadable one is its own
   alert naming what could not be read, never silence and never an all-clear. The watch starts
   nothing: the alert quotes the train's HANDOFF.md, "restart it in its agents:* tmux window".

Five limits on restarting, all mandatory:

* Never during a writeback stall on sami-devbox: a process in state D running `umount` or
  `fusermount3`, or `/proc/pressure/io` full avg10 above 5. A restarter without this rule would
  have made 87 useless resumes on 2026-11-04.
* At most 3 restarts in 10 minutes and 10 in an hour, across every session. Past that it reports
  and stops; it restarts again once the windows empty on their own.
* Never a session that any live process RUNS -- its `--resume`/`--session-id` value, or an
  absolute transcript path on its command line -- even where omp-session-live does not see it,
  so it can never make a duplicate. A live box is never restarted or moved. A process that only
  MENTIONS the id does not count: a headless `omp -p` carries its whole prompt in its arguments,
  and the Verified Linux drift checker's prompt names the lead it reports to, which on
  2026-11-05 made a dead lead look alive (`scripts/tests/test_devbox_offbox_act_live.py`).
* A session that dies again within 30 minutes of its restart is reported, not restarted again; it
  stays blocked until it is seen live.
* Never while that session's box is being relaunched in place. `agentbox restart` hangs omp up
  and spawns a fresh one in the same container, and in that gap the session is dead by every
  reading; at 10:10Z on 2026-11-05 a pass read that and asked for a restart of a session being
  restarted in place, which only `agentbox new`'s duplicate guard stopped. The marker
  agentbox-run.ts writes is a `restarting` reading of the acting half, and it expires after
  BOX_RESTART_SECONDS, since a relaunch that died must not make a session unrestartable.

Every restart and every refusal goes over Envoy to the SRE and to the holder of the session's
role. A restart line names every `--dir` its resume gave the box: a box with too few of them
looks healthy and fails hours later at one path (CURL, 2026-11-04).

Named recipients default to roles, resolved from Envoy's live session list before a direct send.
A send succeeds only with a nonempty event_id and that session's notifications.agent topic.
An unheld role, unavailable hub or unconfirmed receipt is written under
`<state dir>/undelivered/` and logged. The hub runs off the watched box.

`--report-only` (or DEVBOX_OFFBOX_REPORT_ONLY) restarts nothing and sends the SRE one message
naming every session it would have restarted, with its tmux stream target, cwd, --dir list and
resume line. Alerts and refusals go out as usual. It is how the watch runs before a person has
told the stream that automatic restarting is live.

It never kills, signals or stops any process.

Exit status: 0 for a run that reached sami-devbox (quiet or not), 1 when it could not.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
ENVOY = HERE / "envoy"
PROBE = HERE / "devbox-offbox-probe"
ACT = HERE / "devbox-offbox-act"

DEFAULT_HOST = "sami-devbox"
DEFAULT_REGISTRY = "/mnt/durable/sami/restart-registry/sessions"
DEFAULT_TRAIN = "/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/verified-linux/redesign/merge-train"
DEFAULT_SRE = "notifications.role.lead-box-health"
DEFAULT_CHIEF = "notifications.role.chief-of-staff"
DEFAULT_VL_LEAD = "notifications.role.stream-lead-verified-linux"
STATE_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or Path.home() / ".local/state") / "devbox-offbox-watch"

PRESSURE_LIMIT = 5.0          # /proc/pressure/io full avg10 above this is a stall
STALL_ALERT_SECONDS = 600     # a stall that holds this long is an alert of its own
RESTARTS_PER_10_MIN = 3
RESTARTS_PER_HOUR = 10
FLAP_SECONDS = 1800           # dead again within this of its restart: report, never restart again
TRAIN_IDLE_SECONDS = 1800
# How long a restart waits, in its own run, for omp-session-live to say the session is live. It
# is short on purpose: a `oneshot` whose run outlives its 5-minute timer leaves the service in
# `activating` and the timer never fires again -- which is exactly what happened at 19:46:31Z on
# 2026-11-04, when three restarts waited 180 s each and unit-health-watch reported the timer as
# stopped. A session that is not up yet is checked again on the next run, and gets its `after`
# message then.
AWAIT_SECONDS = 45
# No remote call of a run may outlive the unit. devbox-offbox-watch.service sets
# TimeoutStartSec=240; every ssh call below is given a timeout computed against that, so a call
# that hangs ends as a reported failure inside the run instead of systemd killing the run.
#
# Measured on sami-rocqperf on 2026-11-07, from the journal, for the hour to 06:33Z:
#
#   * runs that COMPLETED, restart included, took 40-52 s: 05:52:27->05:53:07, 06:07:28->06:08:18
#     (restart at 06:08:03), 06:22:30->06:23:17, 06:27:30->06:28:22.
#   * runs that were KILLED at 240 s printed no "restarting" line at all: 05:42, 05:47, 05:57,
#     06:02, 06:12, 06:17. They never reached a restart.
#
# So restarts were never the cost. The liveness call was: ask_live was given
# `300 + 3 * len(ids)` -- 504 s at 68 auto_restart entries, more than twice the unit's limit --
# and its box scan blocks on a container that does not answer ("the second liveness test did not
# run (failed 1 container(s) did not answer)", 06:23Z). A call allowed to run for twice the
# unit's life can only ever end by the unit being killed.
#
# An earlier fix of mine budgeted restarts instead, on the reasonable-looking theory that the
# killed runs were the ones restarting the flapping cost lead. The journal says the opposite, and
# the theory survived only because I had not read the run boundaries. The restart guard stays --
# it is correct and bounds several restarts in one pass -- but it was not the bug.
UNIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 240
# Left for cleanup, the state write and the exit after the last remote call returns.
RESTART_MARGIN_SECONDS = 20
# Below this there is no point starting a remote call at all: it would be cut off before it could
# do anything useful, and a cut-off restart is worse than one deferred to the next run.
MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS = 30
ENTRY_KEYS = ("session", "title", "role", "tmux", "cwd", "resume", "after", "order",
              "auto_restart", "owner_note")
STREAM_WINDOWS = ("chief", "ossv", "linux", "tasks", "grader", "infra", "rocq", "cicd")
# The writeback-stall gate reads the facts from the probe on sami-devbox, so the test takes
# them as arguments instead of reading this machine's own /proc.
STALL_COMMS = ("umount", "umount2", "fusermount", "fusermount3")
# How long one session's resume may be held by the gate, counted from the first run that held it.
# A writeback burst that clears itself lasts seconds; the stall of 2026-11-04 lasted about 85
# minutes and ended only with a cloud reset. Twenty minutes is far past the first and far short of
# the second: a box still stalled after it is a person's problem, so at the limit the watch stops
# holding the session, tells the SRE with the exact resume command, and leaves it to them.
STALL_HOLD_MAX = 1200.0


def stall_reason(blocked: list[str], pressure: float) -> str:
    """Why sami-devbox's disk is stalled, with the count of blocked processes, else "".

    ResumeGuard's `writeback_stall`, word for word, with the two facts passed in: the commands of
    the processes sitting in uninterruptible sleep inside an unmount, and /proc/pressure/io's
    `full avg10` -- the share of the last 10 s in which EVERY runnable task waited on IO.
    """
    if not blocked and pressure <= PRESSURE_LIMIT:
        return ""
    what = f" ({', '.join(sorted(set(blocked)))})" if blocked else ""
    return (f"{len(blocked)} process(es) in uninterruptible sleep in an unmount{what},"
            f" IO pressure full avg10 {pressure:.1f} (the stall mark is {PRESSURE_LIMIT:.0f})")


def log(message: str) -> None:
    print(message, flush=True)


def utc(epoch: float) -> str:
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


def minutes(seconds: float) -> int:
    return int(seconds // 60)


def budget_left(run_started: float) -> int:
    """Seconds this run may still spend in a remote call before TimeoutStartSec kills the unit.

    Every ssh call takes its timeout as min(its own, this), so a hung call fails inside the run
    and the run reports it, rather than the run being killed with nothing written. Can be
    negative; callers compare against MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS, not against zero.
    """
    return int(UNIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS - RESTART_MARGIN_SECONDS - (time.monotonic() - run_started))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sami-devbox, over ssh


class Remote:
    """The watched machine, over ssh, in one of two transports.

    **Over stdin** (sami-rocqperf): every call ships its script, so the two halves of this watch
    are never a published version apart.

    **Under a forced command** (the dispatch cluster, dispatch://INFRA-993): the key in
    sami-devbox's authorized_keys carries
    `restrict,command="<pinned path>/devbox-offbox-act --forced"`, and a forced command gets no
    stdin of its own, so nothing can be shipped. The call is then a word or two --
    `probe`, `live <uuid>…`, `restart <uuid>`, `await <uuid>` -- which the pinned
    devbox-offbox-act on the host parses itself and refuses anything else of. The resume line a
    restart runs comes from that host's registry, never from here, so the key cannot ask for an
    arbitrary `agentbox new`.
    """

    def __init__(self, host: str, timeout: int, forced: bool = False, key: str = "") -> None:
        self.host = host
        self.timeout = timeout
        self.forced = forced
        self.key = key

    def _ssh(self, remote: str, stdin: str, timeout: int | None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        command = ["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", f"ConnectTimeout={self.timeout}",
                   "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"]
        if self.key:
            # The pod mounts one key and nothing else; IdentitiesOnly keeps ssh from offering any
            # other identity it may find and being refused for it.
            command += ["-i", self.key, "-o", "IdentitiesOnly=yes"]
        command += [self.host, remote]
        return subprocess.run(command, input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=timeout or (self.timeout + 60))

    def run(self, script: Path, args: list[str], timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        # ssh joins everything after the host into ONE string and hands it to the remote shell,
        # so each word has to carry its own quoting. Without it the first entry whose title held
        # a space and a bracket -- "LINUX-1302 parity owner (specport run record, parity count)",
        # 2026-11-04 19:46:54Z -- turned into a syntax error on the far side instead of a restart.
        remote = " ".join(shlex.quote(word) for word in ["bash", "-s", "--", *args])
        return self._ssh(remote, script.read_text(), timeout)

    def ask(self, words: list[str], timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        """One forced-command call. Every word is a verb or a session id; sshd hands the string
        to devbox-offbox-act --forced, which parses it and never a shell."""
        return self._ssh(" ".join(words), "", timeout)

    # The four things this watch asks of the box, each in whichever transport it has.

    def ask_probe(self, remote_proc: str, registry: str, train: str,
                  timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        if self.forced:
            return self.ask(["probe"], timeout)
        return self.run(PROBE, [remote_proc, registry, train], timeout)

    def ask_live(self, ids: list[str], fresh: int, timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        if self.forced:
            return self.ask(["live", *ids], timeout)
        return self.run(ACT, ["live", str(fresh), *ids], timeout)

    def ask_restart(self, entry: dict[str, str], timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        if self.forced:
            return self.ask(["restart", entry["session"]], timeout)
        tmux, window, _ = placement(entry)
        return self.run(ACT, ["restart", tmux, window, entry["cwd"], entry["resume"]], timeout)

    def ask_await(self, session: str, seconds: int, timeout: int | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        if self.forced:
            return self.ask(["await", session], timeout)
        return self.run(ACT, ["await", session, str(seconds)], timeout)


def parse_probe(text: str) -> tuple[dict[str, str], list[dict[str, str]]]:
    """The probe's `key=value` lines, and one dict per `===ENTRY … ===END` block."""
    facts: dict[str, str] = {}
    entries: list[dict[str, str]] = []
    current: dict[str, str] | None = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("===ENTRY "):
            current = {"_file": line[len("===ENTRY "):]}
            continue
        if line == "===END":
            if current:
                entries.append(current)
            current = None
            continue
        key, sep, value = line.partition("=")
        if not sep:
            continue
        if current is None:
            facts[key] = value
        elif key in ENTRY_KEYS:
            current[key] = value
    return facts, entries


def usable(entry: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    """Why this entry cannot be acted on, or "" when it can."""
    missing = [k for k in ("session", "title", "tmux", "cwd", "resume") if not entry.get(k)]
    if missing:
        return f"no {', '.join(missing)}"
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}",
                        entry["session"]):
        return f"session={entry['session']} is not a session id"
    if entry["_file"] != f"{entry['session']}.entry":
        return f"the file is named {entry['_file']} but holds session={entry['session']}"
    return ""


def placement(entry: dict[str, str]) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
    """Read only tmux=session:stream; unplaced and legacy dev entries use chief."""
    tmux, _, window = entry["tmux"].partition(":")
    if tmux == "dev":
        return "agents", "chief", ""
    invalid = window if window and window not in STREAM_WINDOWS else ""
    return tmux, "chief" if invalid or not window else window, invalid


def dir_list(resume: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every `--dir PATH` the resume command gives the box, in order."""
    try:
        words = shlex.split(resume)
    except ValueError:
        words = resume.split()
    return [words[i + 1] for i, word in enumerate(words[:-1]) if word == "--dir"]


def describe_dirs(resume: str) -> str:
    dirs = dir_list(resume)
    if not dirs:
        return "--dir list used (0): none -- the resume command names no --dir, so this is not a boxed session"
    return f"--dir list used ({len(dirs)}): " + " ".join(dirs)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Envoy


# The marker a log monitor matches. Every alert and every refusal prints one of these lines on
# stdout, whatever becomes of the Envoy message beside it. In the dispatch cluster there is no
# Envoy client in the pod at all and the pod's state directory dies with the pass, so the log line
# is the only thing that leaves a finished pod: a log monitor on this marker posts to #fleet-alerts
# and to the box-health role through the notification path the fleet already has, with no second
# credential for the job (the INFRA lead's call on agent-infra#2194, 2026-11-05). On
# sami-rocqperf, where the client does run, it is one more line in the journal.
ALERT_MARKER = "OFFBOX-ALERT"


class Reporter:
    """Envoy, a structured line in the log for whoever has no Envoy, and a file on this machine for
    what Envoy would not take."""

    def __init__(self, state_dir: Path, dry_run: bool, host: str = "") -> None:
        self.undelivered = state_dir / "undelivered"
        self.dry_run = dry_run
        self.host = host

    def send(self, targets: list[str], kind: str, text: str) -> bool:
        targets = [t for t in dict.fromkeys(targets) if t]
        log(f"[{kind}] -> {', '.join(targets) or 'nobody'}: {text}")
        now = time.time()
        log(f"{ALERT_MARKER} " + json.dumps({
            "at": round(now, 3),
            "iso": utc(now),
            "host": self.host,
            "kind": kind,
            "targets": targets,
            "watcher": socket.gethostname(),
            "dry_run": bool(self.dry_run),
            "text": text,
        }, sort_keys=True))
        if self.dry_run:
            return False  # No receipt exists for a dry run.
        failed = []
        sessions = None
        for target in targets:
            try:
                recipient = target
                if target.startswith("notifications.role."):
                    if sessions is None:
                        listing = subprocess.run([str(ENVOY), "ps", "--json", "--sub-agents"],
                                                 capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
                        if listing.returncode != 0:
                            raise ValueError(f"session list failed: {listing.stderr.strip()}")
                        sessions = json.loads(listing.stdout)
                        if not isinstance(sessions, list):
                            raise ValueError("session list is not a list")
                    role = target.removeprefix("notifications.role.")
                    recipient = next((s["session_id"] for s in sessions
                                      if role in (s.get("roles") or []) and s.get("session_id")), "")
                    if not recipient:
                        raise ValueError(f"role {role} has no live holder")
                done = subprocess.run([str(ENVOY), "send", "--source", "envoy",
                                       "--message-file", "-", recipient],
                                      input=text, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError) as error:
                log(f"  send to {target} failed: {error}")
                failed.append(f"{target}: {error}")
                continue
            if done.returncode != 0:
                detail = (done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()
                log(f"  send to {target} failed: exit {done.returncode}: {detail}")
                failed.append(f"{target}: exit {done.returncode}: {detail}")
                continue
            try:
                answer = json.loads(done.stdout)
            except ValueError:
                answer = None
            if (not isinstance(answer, dict) or not answer.get("event_id")
                    or answer.get("topic") != f"notifications.agent.{recipient}"):
                detail = f"unconfirmed receipt for {recipient}: {done.stdout.strip()[:400]}"
                log(f"  send to {target} failed: {detail}")
                failed.append(f"{target}: {detail}")
            else:
                log(f"  send to {target} confirmed: {answer['event_id']} to {answer['topic']}")
        if failed:
            self.keep(kind, text, failed)
        return bool(targets) and not failed

    def keep(self, kind: str, text: str, failed: list[str]) -> None:
        path = self.undelivered / f"{time.strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ', time.gmtime())}-{kind}.txt"
        try:
            self.undelivered.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            path.write_text("Envoy would not take this message.\n"
                            + "".join(f"  {f}\n" for f in failed) + "\n" + text + "\n")
            log(f"  kept at {path}")
        except OSError as error:
            log(f"  and it could not be kept at {path}: {error}")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State


def load_state(path: Path) -> dict:
    try:
        state = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        state = {}
    state.setdefault("ssh_fail_streak", 0)
    state.setdefault("ssh_alerted", False)
    state.setdefault("ssh_last_error", "")
    state.setdefault("stall_since", None)
    state.setdefault("stall_alerted", False)
    state.setdefault("restarts", [])
    state.setdefault("sessions", {})
    state.setdefault("train", {})
    return state


def save_state(path: Path, state: dict) -> None:
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(state, indent=1, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    tmp.replace(path)


def session_state(state: dict, session: str) -> dict:
    return state["sessions"].setdefault(session, {})


def once(record: dict, key: str, on: bool) -> bool:
    """True the first time `key` is on, then False until it has been off. The dedupe behind
    "report every refusal" that keeps a standing refusal from being a message every 5 minutes."""
    was = bool(record.get(key))
    record[key] = on
    return on and not was


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The run


def check_box(facts: dict[str, str], state: dict, report: Reporter, now: float,
              host: str, sre: str, chief: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Is sami-devbox in a writeback stall? Alerts when one holds for STALL_ALERT_SECONDS."""
    try:
        pressure = float(facts.get("pressure_io_full_avg10", ""))
    except ValueError:
        pressure = 0.0
    blocked = [entry.split(":", 1)[1] for entry in facts.get("writeback_d_procs", "").split(",")
               if ":" in entry]
    detail = stall_reason(blocked, pressure)
    stalled = bool(detail)
    if not stalled:
        if state["stall_alerted"]:
            report.send([sre, chief], "stall-over",
                        f"{host} is out of its writeback stall as of {utc(now)}"
                        f" (/proc/pressure/io full avg10={pressure}, no umount or fusermount3 in state D)."
                        f" devbox-offbox-watch is restarting registry sessions again.")
        state["stall_since"] = None
        state["stall_alerted"] = False
        return False, ""
    if state["stall_since"] is None:
        state["stall_since"] = now
    held = now - state["stall_since"]
    if held >= STALL_ALERT_SECONDS and not state["stall_alerted"]:
        state["stall_alerted"] = True
        report.send([sre, chief], "stall",
                    f"{host} WRITEBACK STALL: it answers ssh but has been stalled for"
                    f" {minutes(held)} min, since {utc(state['stall_since'])}. Evidence: {detail}."
                    f" devbox-offbox-watch is refusing every restart while it holds -- a resume now"
                    f" would hang the way the 87 of 2026-11-04 did -- and it has killed nothing."
                    f" It reports again when the stall clears.")
    return True, detail


# The three standing merge-train processes, with the pattern that finds each one. They are the
# three the box's own liveness job watched (`operator/train_alive.sh`, run by the stream job
# `merge-train-alive`), which this check replaces: a job armed ON sami-devbox dies with the box
# it watches, which is the whole reason this watch runs off it.
TRAIN_PROCESSES = {
    "revert_watch": "operator/revert_watch.py",
    "nightly_accept_load": "operator/nightly_accept_load.py",
    "snapshot_loop": "operator/snapshot.sh --loop",
}

# What the train's HANDOFF.md tells its operator to do, quoted rather than paraphrased. This
# watch starts nothing: decision 37 keeps the five processes in their own tmux windows.
TRAIN_RESTART = ("restart it in its agents:* tmux window, per"
                 " redesign/merge-train/HANDOFF.md. This watch starts nothing and has killed"
                 " nothing.")


def check_train(facts: dict[str, str], state: dict, report: Reporter, now: float,
                train_dir: str, sre: str, vl_lead: str) -> None:
    train = state["train"]
    lock = facts.get("train_lock", "")
    operator = facts.get("train_lock_agent", "")
    open_items = facts.get("train_queue_open", "0")
    targets = [operator, vl_lead, sre]
    present = facts.get("train_present", "no")
    if present != "yes":
        # A train directory that is there and cannot be read is its own alert: a probe that
        # could not look must never be read as a quiet train. A directory that is not there at
        # all is how this watch runs where there is no train, and stays silent.
        if once(train, "unreadable", present == "unreadable"):
            report.send([vl_lead, sre], "train-unreadable",
                        f"MERGE TRAIN NOT READABLE: {train_dir} exists on the box and"
                        f" devbox-offbox-probe could not read it, so NOTHING about the train was"
                        f" checked this pass -- not the runner, not the gate, not the three"
                        f" standing processes. This is a could-not-look, not an all-clear.")
        return
    rule = ("Rule used: this watch's brief -- the queue holds work and events.tsv has had no new"
            " line for 30 min, or runner.lock names a pid that is not running. The train's own"
            " README states no stall condition of its own; its only word on a stopped runner is"
            " the \"Taking over\" procedure, which this alert quotes.")

    alive = facts.get("train_lock_pid_alive", "unknown")
    if once(train, "lock_dead", alive == "no"):
        report.send(targets, "train-runner-gone",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN RUNNER GONE: {train_dir}/runner.lock names a pid that is not"
                    f" running on the box. Lock: {lock or '(empty)'}. {open_items} queue item(s)"
                    f" are open. To take over, per the train's README (\"Taking over\"): start"
                    f" `TRAIN_AGENT=<your id> ./train.sh run` as a background job with no deadline"
                    f" in that directory; it gets the lock once the old process is gone, adopts the"
                    f" cycle if the head has not moved, and otherwise requeues the testing items."
                    f" Then relay the outbox after each landing. {rule}")

    # The gate lock and the three standing processes, each in three states. "Could not look" is
    # not "it is dead": an unreadable reading gets its own alert below and is never counted as
    # alive, which is the whole failure mode a liveness check has.
    gate = facts.get("train_gate_pid_alive", "unreadable")
    gate_was_dead = bool(train.get("gate_dead"))
    train["gate_dead"] = gate == "no"
    if gate == "no" and not gate_was_dead:
        report.send(targets, "train-gate-gone",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN GATE GONE: {train_dir}/gate.lock names a pid that is not"
                    f" running on the box. Lock: {facts.get('train_gate_lock', '') or '(empty)'}."
                    f" {open_items} queue item(s) are open. The gate is the train's test gate:"
                    f" {TRAIN_RESTART}")
    elif gate_was_dead and gate == "yes":
        report.send(targets, "train-gate-back",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN GATE BACK: {train_dir}/gate.lock names a running pid again"
                    f" ({facts.get('train_gate_lock', '') or '(empty)'}).")

    missing = sorted(name for name in TRAIN_PROCESSES
                     if facts.get(f"train_proc_{name}") == "no")
    previously = list(train.get("procs_missing") or [])
    train["procs_missing"] = missing
    if missing and missing != previously:
        # One alert per episode, naming the whole missing set; a set that grows or shrinks is a
        # new fact and is said again, and an unchanged one is not.
        gone = ", ".join(TRAIN_PROCESSES[name] for name in missing)
        report.send(targets, "train-procs-gone",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN PROCESS GONE: no process on the box matches {gone}"
                    f" ({len(missing)} of {len(TRAIN_PROCESSES)} standing train processes)."
                    f" Train dir {train_dir}; {open_items} queue item(s) are open. For each:"
                    f" {TRAIN_RESTART}")
    elif previously and not missing:
        report.send(targets, "train-procs-back",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN PROCESSES BACK: all {len(TRAIN_PROCESSES)} standing train"
                    f" processes are running again (was:"
                    f" {', '.join(TRAIN_PROCESSES[name] for name in previously)}).")

    unreadable = [f"gate.lock ({train_dir}/gate.lock)"] if gate == "unreadable" else []
    unreadable += [TRAIN_PROCESSES[name] for name in TRAIN_PROCESSES
                   if facts.get(f"train_proc_{name}") == "unreadable"]
    if once(train, "unreadable", bool(unreadable)):
        report.send([vl_lead, sre], "train-unreadable",
                    f"MERGE TRAIN CHECK COULD NOT LOOK: devbox-offbox-probe could not read"
                    f" {'; '.join(unreadable)} on the box, so those facts are unknown this pass"
                    f" -- not alive, not dead. Everything else about the train was checked"
                    f" normally. Nothing was touched.")

    mtime = facts.get("train_events_mtime", "")
    if mtime and open_items.isdigit() and int(open_items) > 0:
        idle = now - float(mtime)
        if once(train, "events_idle", idle >= TRAIN_IDLE_SECONDS):
            report.send(targets, "train-stuck",
                        f"MERGE TRAIN STUCK: {train_dir} has {open_items} open queue item(s)"
                        f" ({facts.get('train_queue_states', '').strip()}) and events.tsv has had no"
                        f" new line for {minutes(idle)} min -- its last is from"
                        f" {utc(float(mtime))}: {facts.get('train_events_last', '')}."
                        f" runner.lock: {lock or '(empty)'}. Nothing was touched. {rule}")
    else:
        train["events_idle"] = False


# How recently the transcript must have been written to count on its own. A session waiting on a
# person writes nothing for hours, so this is a positive signal only; the descriptor holder is
# the test that answers for a quiet session.
FRESH_SECONDS = 120

# How long a box's relaunch marker counts. `agentbox restart` hangs omp up and spawns a fresh one
# in the same container; in that gap the session is dead by every reading this watch has, and at
# 10:10Z on 2026-11-05 a pass read exactly that and asked the host to restart a session UTIL was
# restarting in place -- only `agentbox new`'s own duplicate guard stopped there being two of it.
# agentbox-run.ts writes the marker before the hang-up and removes it when the new omp registers.
# The number is 180 s: this machine's own box logs
# (~/.cache/omp/agentbox/*.log, every restart request and its relaunch) hold 8 relaunches, 0.2 s
# to 4.1 s from the hang-up to the new omp (median 0.6 s), so 180 s is more than forty times the
# slowest one and covers the startup after it. It EXPIRES on purpose: a relaunch that died leaves
# the marker behind, and a session nothing may ever restart again is worse than one restarted
# twice -- the registration the marker waits for does not always come (on 2026-11-04 several
# resumed sessions did not register within the waker's 600 s).
BOX_RESTART_SECONDS = 180


def liveness(remote: Remote, ids: list[str], run_started: float) -> tuple[dict[str, list[str]], dict[str, str], dict[str, int], str]:
    """Every reading sami-devbox has about each session, and whether the box scan can be trusted.

    Returns, per id, the reasons it is alive (strongest first); separately the host and box
    processes that merely name it; separately again the age in seconds of a box relaunch in
    flight for it; and "" when every agent box answered.

    The file is the test that works. At 21:26Z on 2026-11-04 session 01a1a7fc was writing its
    transcript and sending Envoy messages while no process on the host and none inside any of the
    111 running boxes carried its id on a command line: it had been started from a transcript
    path through a wrapper. Every command-line test is blind to that, so a session counts as
    alive when something holds its transcript open for writing, or the transcript moved in the
    last FRESH_SECONDS, or any of the three weaker signals fires.

    A `restarting` line is not one of those. A session whose box is mid-relaunch is genuinely
    dead at that instant, so it is not called alive; it is a refusal with its own message, and
    the next pass after the relaunch finishes finds it live by the readings above.
    """
    if not ids:
        return {}, {}, {}, ""
    # One `omp-session-live` per id (about 0.75 s), one `ps` and one descriptor pass on the host,
    # and the box scan: 24 s over 116 containers, measured 2026-11-04.
    #
    # Bounded by what is left of the unit's life. `300 + 3 * len(ids)` is 504 s at 68 entries,
    # which cannot be reached inside a 240 s unit: a call allowed to outlive its unit does not
    # time out, it gets the unit killed. Six runs in the 90 minutes to 06:33Z on 2026-11-07 ended
    # that way, each hung on a box that does not answer the scan. With this, the call returns and
    # the run says which part failed.
    done = remote.ask_live(ids, FRESH_SECONDS,
                           timeout=min(300 + 3 * len(ids), max(budget_left(run_started), MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS)))
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"liveness check failed: exit {done.returncode}:"
                           f" {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()[:400]}")
    alive: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    named: dict[str, str] = {}
    restarting: dict[str, int] = {}
    scan = "the box scan printed no verdict"
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split(" ", 3)
        if parts[0] == "holder" and len(parts) >= 4:
            where = "on the host" if parts[2] == "host" else f"in box {parts[2]}"
            alive.setdefault(parts[1], []).append(f"process {parts[3]} {where} holds its transcript open")
        elif parts[0] == "fresh" and len(parts) >= 4:
            alive.setdefault(parts[1], []).append(f"its transcript {parts[3]} was written {parts[2]} s ago")
        elif parts[0] == "proc" and len(parts) >= 4:
            alive.setdefault(parts[1], []).append(f"host process {parts[2]} names it")
            named[parts[1]] = f"pid {parts[2]}: {parts[3]}"
        elif parts[0] == "box" and len(parts) >= 4:
            alive.setdefault(parts[1], []).append(f"a process in box {parts[2]} names it")
            named[parts[1]] = f"box {parts[2]}: {parts[3]}"
        elif parts[0] == "live" and len(parts) >= 3 and parts[2] == "1":
            alive.setdefault(parts[1], []).append("omp-session-live says it is live")
        elif parts[0] == "restarting" and len(parts) >= 3 and parts[2].lstrip("-").isdigit():
            restarting[parts[1]] = int(parts[2])
        elif parts[0] == "boxscan":
            scan = "" if parts[1] == "ok" else " ".join(parts[1:])
    return alive, named, restarting, scan


def restart(remote: Remote, entry: dict[str, str], report: Reporter, state: dict, now: float,
            host: str, sre: str, dry_run: bool, run_started: float) -> None:
    session, title = entry["session"], entry["title"]
    targets = [sre] + ([f"notifications.role.{entry['role']}"] if entry.get("role") else [])
    dirs = describe_dirs(entry["resume"])
    tmux, stream, _ = placement(entry)
    log(f"restarting {title} ({session}) in tmux {tmux}:{stream}, cwd {entry['cwd']}: {dirs}")
    if dry_run:
        log("  --dry-run: nothing was run")
        return
    # The attempt counts against the limits before it is made, whether or not it works: a resume
    # line that fails every time is exactly the loop the limits exist to stop.
    state["restarts"].append({"at": now, "session": session})
    record = session_state(state, session)
    record["last_restart"] = now
    record["last_dirs"] = dir_list(entry["resume"])
    # Bounded like every other remote call: 180 s is the cost of a slow box creation, but never
    # more than this run may still spend.
    done = remote.ask_restart(entry, timeout=min(180, max(budget_left(run_started), MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS)))
    if done.returncode != 0:
        detail = (done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()[:500]
        report.send(targets, "restart-failed",
                    f"RESTART FAILED for {title} ({session}) on {host}: the tmux pane could not"
                    f" be opened in {tmux}:{stream}: {detail}. Resume line: {entry['resume']}. {dirs}."
                    f" Nothing is running for this session; it needs a person.")
        return
    window = pane = ""
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("window="):
            window = line[len("window="):]
        elif line.startswith("pane="):
            pane = line[len("pane="):]

    waited = remote.ask_await(session, AWAIT_SECONDS,
                              timeout=min(AWAIT_SECONDS + 60, max(budget_left(run_started), MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS)))
    came_back = waited.stdout.strip() == "live"
    after = entry.get("after", "")
    if came_back and after:
        confirmed = report.send([session], "after-message", after)
        after_said = "its after message was sent" if confirmed else "its after message is UNCONFIRMED"
        record["pending_after"] = not confirmed
    elif after:
        after_said = ("its after message is not sent yet; the next run sends it as soon as"
                      " omp-session-live sees the session live")
        record["pending_after"] = True
    else:
        after_said = "it has no after message"
    live_said = ("omp-session-live sees it live" if came_back else
                 f"omp-session-live has not seen it live within {AWAIT_SECONDS} s; the next run"
                 f" looks again, and reports it as dead-again if it is still not up")
    recent = len([r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600])
    report.send(targets, "restart",
                f"RESTARTED {title} ({session}) on {host} at {utc(now)}. It was dead by"
                f" omp-session-live, and no process on the box named it. New tmux pane"
                f" {pane or '(not reported)'} in {window or f'{tmux}:{stream}'}, cwd {entry['cwd']}."
                f" {dirs}. Resume line run: {entry['resume']}. Now: {live_said}, and {after_said}."
                f" Restarts in the last hour: {recent} of {RESTARTS_PER_HOUR}."
                f" Why it matters: {entry.get('owner_note', '(the entry says nothing)')}")


def run(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """One pass, and its one line in the shadow log however the pass ended."""
    shadow: dict = {"at": round(time.time(), 3), "iso": utc(time.time())}
    try:
        return run_pass(args, shadow)
    finally:
        write_shadow(args, shadow)


def run_pass(args: argparse.Namespace, shadow: dict) -> int:
    now = time.time()
    # Wall clock for the restart budget below. monotonic, not `now`: a clock step must not hand a
    # run more room than TimeoutStartSec actually gives it.
    run_started = time.monotonic()
    state_path = Path(args.state)
    state = load_state(state_path)
    report = Reporter(state_path.parent, args.dry_run, args.host)
    remote = Remote(args.host, args.ssh_timeout, forced=args.forced_command, key=args.ssh_key)
    me = socket.gethostname()

    try:
        probe = remote.ask_probe(args.remote_proc, args.registry, args.train,
                                 timeout=args.ssh_timeout + 60)
        if probe.returncode != 0:
            raise RuntimeError(f"exit {probe.returncode}: {(probe.stderr or probe.stdout).strip()[:300]}")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, RuntimeError) as error:
        state["ssh_fail_streak"] += 1
        state["ssh_last_error"] = str(error)[:300]
        log(f"{args.host} did not answer: {error}")
        if state["ssh_fail_streak"] >= 2 and not state["ssh_alerted"]:
            state["ssh_alerted"] = True
            report.send([args.sre, args.chief], "box-down",
                        f"{args.host} DOWN: it has not answered ssh within {args.ssh_timeout} s on"
                        f" {state['ssh_fail_streak']} runs in a row, the last at {utc(now)}"
                        f" ({state['ssh_last_error']}). Nothing on it can be restarted and nothing"
                        f" on it is watching itself. devbox-offbox-watch keeps asking every 5 min"
                        f" from {me} and reports when it answers again.")
        save_state(state_path, state)
        return 1

    if state["ssh_alerted"]:
        report.send([args.sre, args.chief], "box-up",
                    f"{args.host} answers ssh again as of {utc(now)}, after"
                    f" {state['ssh_fail_streak']} failed runs. devbox-offbox-watch is checking the"
                    f" registry's sessions again.")
    state["ssh_fail_streak"] = 0
    state["ssh_alerted"] = False

    shadow["reached"] = "probed"
    facts, entries = parse_probe(probe.stdout)
    stalled, stall_detail = check_box(facts, state, report, now, args.host, args.sre, args.chief)
    check_train(facts, state, report, now, args.train, args.sre, args.verified_linux_lead)

    if facts.get("registry_present") != "yes":
        shadow["reached"] = "no-registry"
        log(f"no registry at {args.registry} on {args.host}: nothing to watch")
        save_state(state_path, state)
        return 0

    good: list[dict[str, str]] = []
    for entry in entries:
        why = usable(entry)
        if why:
            log(f"skipping registry file {entry.get('_file')}: {why}")
            continue
        good.append(entry)
    log(f"{len(good)} registry entries, {sum(1 for e in good if e.get('auto_restart') == 'yes')}"
        f" with auto_restart=yes")

    watched = [e for e in good if e.get("auto_restart") == "yes"
               or session_state(state, e["session"]).get("blocked")]
    watched.sort(key=lambda e: (int(e["order"]) if e.get("order", "").lstrip("-").isdigit() else 999,
                                e["title"]))
    try:
        alive, named, restarting, scan_failed = liveness(remote, [e["session"] for e in watched],
                                                         run_started)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, RuntimeError) as error:
        log(f"liveness check failed: {error}")
        save_state(state_path, state)
        return 1
    if scan_failed:
        log(f"the agentbox scan could not be trusted ({scan_failed}); nothing is called dead this run")

    shadow.update({
        "reached": "judged",
        "watched": [e["session"] for e in watched],
        "live": sorted(alive),
        "named": sorted(named),
        "restarting": dict(sorted(restarting.items())),
        "stalled": bool(stalled),
        "scan_failed": scan_failed,
    })
    dead = []
    for entry in watched:
        session = entry["session"]
        record = session_state(state, session)
        # Alive on ANY reading, with the transcript first. A command line that does not carry the
        # id proves nothing: session 01a1a7fc was writing its transcript at 21:26Z on 2026-11-04
        # while no process anywhere, host or box, named it.
        reasons = alive.get(session) or []
        if reasons:
            record["last_seen_live"] = now
            log(f"{entry['title']} ({session}) is live: {reasons[0]}")
            if record.get("blocked"):
                log(f"{entry['title']} ({session}) is live again; unblocked")
            # A restart whose session was not up inside its own run leaves the `after` message
            # here, so it is sent on the first run that finds the session live rather than
            # holding a run open waiting for it.
            if record.get("pending_after") and entry.get("after"):
                log(f"{entry['title']} ({session}) is live; sending the after message it was owed")
                confirmed = report.send([session], "after-message", entry["after"])
                if confirmed:
                    targets = [args.sre] + ([f"notifications.role.{entry['role']}"] if entry.get("role") else [])
                    report.send(targets, "after-sent",
                                f"{entry['title']} ({session}) came up after its restart and has now"
                                f" been sent its after message: {entry['after']}")
                record["pending_after"] = not confirmed
            record["blocked"] = False
            record["blocked_reported"] = False
            record["duplicate_reported"] = False
            continue
        dead.append(entry)
    shadow["dead"] = sorted(e["session"] for e in dead)

    if not dead:
        log("every watched session is live")
        state["restarts"] = [r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600]
        save_state(state_path, state)
        return 0

    if scan_failed:
        # "Could not test" is never "dead". One of the two tests did not run, so this run reports
        # and restarts nothing at all.
        names = ", ".join(f"{e['title']} ({e['session']})" for e in dead)
        if once(state, "scan_failed_reported", True):
            report.send([args.sre], "refuse-untestable",
                        f"NOT RESTARTED, {len(dead)} session(s) that look dead on {args.host}:"
                        f" {names}. The second liveness test did not run ({scan_failed}), and a box"
                        f" that cannot be asked is not an empty box. A resumed copy of a session"
                        f" that is actually alive takes its Envoy registration and leaves the"
                        f" original unaddressable, so this run restarts nothing. It tries again"
                        f" in 5 minutes.")
        log(f"box scan not trusted; {len(dead)} apparently dead session(s) left alone")
        state["restarts"] = [r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600]
        save_state(state_path, state)
        return 0
    state["scan_failed_reported"] = False

    left_for_stall: list[str] = []
    left_for_limit: list[str] = []
    # Sessions this run had no wall-clock room left to restart; the next run takes them.
    left_for_budget: list[str] = []
    left_for_box_restart: list[str] = []
    would_restart: list[str] = []
    for entry in dead:
        session, title = entry["session"], entry["title"]
        record = session_state(state, session)
        if not stalled:
            record.pop("stall_hold_since", None)
            record["stall_handover_reported"] = False
        targets = [args.sre] + ([f"notifications.role.{entry['role']}"] if entry.get("role") else [])

        # The fifth refusal: this session's box is being relaunched right now. `agentbox restart`
        # hangs omp up and spawns a fresh one in the same container, and for those seconds the
        # session is dead by every reading. At 10:10Z on 2026-11-05 a pass read exactly that and
        # asked the host to restart a session UTIL was restarting in place; only `agentbox new`'s
        # own duplicate guard stopped there being two of it, and a guard should not be the only
        # thing between us and a duplicate. The marker expires (BOX_RESTART_SECONDS), so a
        # relaunch that died cannot make a session unrestartable.
        age = restarting.get(session)
        if age is not None and age < BOX_RESTART_SECONDS:
            left_for_box_restart.append(f"{title} ({session}), {age} s into its relaunch")
            log(f"{title} ({session}) is mid-relaunch ({age} s): not restarted")
            continue
        if age is not None:
            log(f"{title} ({session}) has a relaunch marker {age} s old, past"
                f" {BOX_RESTART_SECONDS} s: it is treated as dead")

        held = [named[session]] if session in named else []
        if held:
            record["blocked"] = True
            if once(record, "duplicate_reported", True):
                report.send(targets, "refuse-duplicate",
                            f"NOT RESTARTED {title} ({session}): omp-session-live says it is dead,"
                            f" but {len(held)} live process(es) on {args.host} name it"
                            f" -- {'; '.join(held[:5])}. A restart could make a duplicate, so none"
                            f" was made and nothing was killed. This needs a person: either the"
                            f" session is coming up, or something of it is left behind."
                            f" Why it matters: {entry.get('owner_note', '(the entry says nothing)')}")
            continue

        since = now - float(record.get("last_restart") or 0)
        if record.get("last_restart") and since < FLAP_SECONDS:
            record["blocked"] = True
            if once(record, "blocked_reported", True):
                dirs = record.get("last_dirs") or []
                report.send(targets, "refuse-flap",
                            f"NOT RESTARTED {title} ({session}): it died again {minutes(since)} min"
                            f" after devbox-offbox-watch restarted it at"
                            f" {utc(record['last_restart'])}. A second restart is not made."
                            f" It stays blocked until omp-session-live sees it live, so a person"
                            f" decides: its resume line may be wrong, or whatever killed it is"
                            f" still there. --dir list the restart used ({len(dirs)}):"
                            f" {' '.join(dirs) if dirs else 'none'}. Resume line: {entry['resume']}."
                            f" Why it matters: {entry.get('owner_note', '(the entry says nothing)')}")
            continue
        if record.get("blocked"):
            continue

        if stalled:
            # ResumeGuard's limit: a session is held by the stall gate for at most 20 minutes.
            # Past that the box is a person's problem, so the hold ends, the SRE gets the exact
            # resume command, and this watch does not resume it later on its own.
            record.setdefault("stall_hold_since", now)
            holding = now - record["stall_hold_since"]
            if holding >= STALL_HOLD_MAX:
                record["blocked"] = True
                record.pop("stall_hold_since", None)
                if once(record, "stall_handover_reported", True):
                    report.send(targets, "stall-handover",
                                f"HELD TOO LONG, now yours: {title} ({session}) has been dead and"
                                f" held by the writeback-stall gate for {minutes(holding)} min"
                                f" ({stall_detail}). devbox-offbox-watch stops holding it and"
                                f" will not resume it on its own. Resume it by hand when"
                                f" {args.host} is writing again, from {entry['cwd']}, in tmux"
                                f" {entry['tmux']}: {entry['resume']}."
                                f" {describe_dirs(entry['resume'])}."
                                f" Why it matters: {entry.get('owner_note', '(the entry says nothing)')}")
                continue
            left_for_stall.append(f"{title} ({session})")
            continue

        ten = len([r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 600])
        hour = len([r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600])
        if ten >= RESTARTS_PER_10_MIN or hour >= RESTARTS_PER_HOUR:
            left_for_limit.append(f"{title} ({session})")
            continue

        tmux, stream, invalid = placement(entry)
        if invalid and record.get("refused_window") != invalid:
            report.send(targets, "refuse-window",
                        f"REFUSED WINDOW for {title} ({session}): tmux={entry['tmux']} does not name"
                        f" one of {', '.join(STREAM_WINDOWS)}. Its restart uses {tmux}:chief instead;"
                        f" no window with the refused name is created. Correct the registry entry.")
            record["refused_window"] = invalid
        elif not invalid:
            record.pop("refused_window", None)

        if args.report_only:
            would_restart.append(f"{title} ({session}), tmux {tmux}:{stream}, cwd {entry['cwd']},"
                                 f" {describe_dirs(entry['resume'])}, resume: {entry['resume']}")
            log(f"report-only: would restart {title} ({session})")
            continue

        # Start this restart only while the run has time to do something useful with it. A
        # restart cut off halfway is worse than one deferred: it counts against the rate limits,
        # may leave a half-made box, and the session is unrestarted either way.
        left = budget_left(run_started)
        if left < MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS:
            left_for_budget.append(f"{title} ({session})")
            log(f"deferring the restart of {title} ({session}) to the next run:"
                f" {left} s left of the unit's {UNIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS} s, under the"
                f" {MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS} s a restart needs to be worth starting")
            continue

        restart(remote, entry, report, state, now, args.host, args.sre, args.dry_run, run_started)

    if would_restart:
        report.send([args.sre], "report-only",
                    f"REPORT ONLY, nothing was restarted. {len(would_restart)} session(s) of the"
                    f" registry are dead on {args.host} and devbox-offbox-watch would have brought"
                    f" each back: " + " | ".join(would_restart)
                    + f". It is in report-only mode and restarts nothing until that is turned off"
                      f" (`--report-only`, or DEVBOX_OFFBOX_REPORT_ONLY in"
                      f" ~/.config/systemd/user/devbox-offbox-watch.service.d/report-only.conf on"
                      f" sami-rocqperf).")

    if once(state, "stall_refusal_reported", bool(left_for_stall)):
        report.send([args.sre], "refuse-stall",
                    f"NOT RESTARTED, {len(left_for_stall)} dead session(s) on {args.host}:"
                    f" {', '.join(left_for_stall)}. The box is in a writeback stall"
                    f" ({stall_detail}), where a resume hangs instead of starting -- a restarter"
                    f" without this rule would have made 87 useless resumes on 2026-11-04."
                    f" They are restarted on the first run after the stall clears. Nothing was"
                    f" killed.")
    if once(state, "limit_reported", bool(left_for_limit)):
        ten = len([r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 600])
        hour = len([r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600])
        report.send([args.sre], "refuse-limit",
                    f"NOT RESTARTED, {len(left_for_limit)} dead session(s) on {args.host}:"
                    f" {', '.join(left_for_limit)}. The restart limit is reached: {ten} restart(s)"
                    f" in the last 10 min (limit {RESTARTS_PER_10_MIN}) and {hour} in the last hour"
                    f" (limit {RESTARTS_PER_HOUR}). devbox-offbox-watch has stopped restarting."
                    f" It starts again by itself once those windows empty, so if this repeats,"
                    f" something is resuming in a loop or the box is broken: look before it does.")
    # Deliberately not wrapped in `once`: each run that defers names what it deferred, because a
    # deferral is only harmless if the next run picks it up, and a silent one would hide a session
    # that never gets its turn.
    if left_for_budget:
        report.send([args.sre], "defer-budget",
                    f"DEFERRED TO THE NEXT RUN, {len(left_for_budget)} dead session(s) on"
                    f" {args.host}: {', '.join(left_for_budget)}. This run had no wall-clock room"
                    f" left: under {MIN_USEFUL_SECONDS} s remained of the unit's"
                    f" {UNIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS} s (TimeoutStartSec). Nothing is wrong with"
                    f" these sessions; the next run in 5 min restarts them. If the SAME session is"
                    f" deferred run after run, the liveness leg has outgrown the budget -- measure"
                    f" a run and either make it cheaper or raise both limits together.")
    if once(state, "box_restart_reported", bool(left_for_box_restart)):
        report.send([args.sre], "refuse-box-restart",
                    f"NOT RESTARTED, {len(left_for_box_restart)} dead session(s) on {args.host}:"
                    f" {'; '.join(left_for_box_restart)}. Each one's box is being relaunched in"
                    f" place right now (`agentbox restart` hangs omp up and spawns a fresh one in"
                    f" the same container, and in that gap the session is dead by every reading"
                    f" this watch has). A restart from here would have made a second copy of a"
                    f" session that is coming back on its own; at 10:10Z on 2026-11-05 only"
                    f" `agentbox new`'s own duplicate guard stopped that. The marker expires after"
                    f" {BOX_RESTART_SECONDS} s, so a relaunch that died leaves the session"
                    f" restartable. Nothing was killed.")

    shadow["would_restart"] = sorted(e.split("(")[-1].split(")")[0] for e in would_restart)
    shadow["refused"] = {"stall": sorted(left_for_stall), "limit": sorted(left_for_limit),
                         "box_restart": sorted(left_for_box_restart)}
    state["restarts"] = [r for r in state["restarts"] if now - r["at"] < 3600]
    save_state(state_path, state)
    return 0


def write_shadow(args: argparse.Namespace, facts: dict) -> None:
    """One JSON line per pass, so this watch can be compared with another one running beside it.

    The cluster watcher runs for a day in report-only mode next to the sami-rocqperf one, and the
    INFRA lead arms it only after a day in which the two agreed on every pass
    (dispatch://INFRA-993). `devbox-offbox-shadow-compare` reads two of these files and says, pass
    by pass, whether they called the same sessions dead and would have restarted the same ones.
    EVERY pass writes a line, the quiet ones included: a day in which the two watchers saw nothing
    to do, and agreed on that, is most of the evidence the arming condition asks for.

    `--shadow-log -` writes the line to stdout with the prefix `SHADOW-PASS `, which is how a
    pod's pass reaches a reader: its log is all there is of a finished pod.
    """
    if not args.shadow_log:
        return
    record = {
        "at": 0.0, "iso": "", "watcher": socket.gethostname(), "host": args.host,
        "mode": ("dry-run" if args.dry_run else "report-only" if args.report_only else "restarting"),
        "forced_command": bool(args.forced_command),
        # What the pass reached: `ssh-down` and `no-registry` are passes with no verdict in them,
        # which the comparison counts as agreeing only with another pass of the same shape.
        "reached": "ssh-down", "watched": [], "live": [], "dead": [], "named": [],
        "restarting": {}, "stalled": False, "scan_failed": "", "would_restart": [],
        "refused": {"stall": [], "limit": [], "box_restart": []},
    }
    record.update(facts)
    line = json.dumps(record, sort_keys=True)
    if args.shadow_log == "-":
        log(f"SHADOW-PASS {line}")
        return
    path = Path(args.shadow_log)
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with path.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write(line + "\n")
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"could not write the shadow log {path}: {error}")


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("--host", default=DEFAULT_HOST, help="the machine this one watches")
    parser.add_argument("--registry", default=DEFAULT_REGISTRY, help="restart registry on --host")
    parser.add_argument("--train", default=DEFAULT_TRAIN, help="merge train directory on --host")
    parser.add_argument("--remote-proc", default="/proc",
                        help="procfs the stall test reads on --host; a private tree for the tests")
    parser.add_argument("--sre", default=os.environ.get("BOX_HEALTH_OWNER") or DEFAULT_SRE,
                        help="box-health role topic or explicit session id")
    parser.add_argument("--chief", default=os.environ.get("OFFBOX_CHIEF") or DEFAULT_CHIEF,
                        help="chief of staff role topic or explicit session id")
    parser.add_argument("--verified-linux-lead", default=os.environ.get("OFFBOX_VL_LEAD") or DEFAULT_VL_LEAD,
                        help="Verified Linux lead role topic or explicit session id")
    parser.add_argument("--ssh-timeout", type=int, default=30)
    parser.add_argument("--state", default=str(STATE_DIR / "state.json"))
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="say what would happen; start nothing and send nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--report-only", action="store_true",
                        default=bool(os.environ.get("DEVBOX_OFFBOX_REPORT_ONLY")),
                        help="restart nothing; send the SRE one message naming what would have"
                             " been restarted. Alerts and refusals are sent as usual."
                             " DEVBOX_OFFBOX_REPORT_ONLY sets it too")
    parser.add_argument("--once", action="store_true", help="accepted and ignored; a run is one pass")
    parser.add_argument("--forced-command", action="store_true",
                        default=bool(os.environ.get("DEVBOX_OFFBOX_FORCED_COMMAND")),
                        help="reach --host through the forced command of a dedicated key: every"
                             " call is `probe`, `live <uuid>…`, `restart <uuid>` or"
                             " `await <uuid>`, and no script is shipped over stdin."
                             " DEVBOX_OFFBOX_FORCED_COMMAND sets it too")
    parser.add_argument("--ssh-key", default=os.environ.get("DEVBOX_OFFBOX_SSH_KEY", ""),
                        help="the private key to reach --host with, and only it"
                             " (IdentitiesOnly); the cluster's pod mounts one")
    parser.add_argument("--shadow-log", default=os.environ.get("DEVBOX_OFFBOX_SHADOW_LOG", ""),
                        help="append one JSON line per pass here for"
                             " devbox-offbox-shadow-compare; `-` writes it to stdout as"
                             " `SHADOW-PASS <json>`, which is how a pod's pass is read back")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    mode = ("report-only, restarting nothing" if args.report_only else
            "dry run, changing and sending nothing" if args.dry_run else "restarting")
    log(f"devbox-offbox-watch on {socket.gethostname()} at {utc(time.time())},"
        f" watching {args.host} ({mode})")
    return run(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
